## Why This Is Asked
This probes multi-tenant governance, scale, streaming semantics, schema drift handling, and retention policies across a large lakehouse. It also tests trade-offs between Spark and Flink for high-volume streaming and the practicality of per-tenant partitioning and access control.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant data contracts and lineage
- Exactly-once streaming and idempotent upserts
- Iceberg partitioning by tenant/region
- Schema drift handling and canonical schema
- Data retention, GDPR-style deletes, and privacy masking

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: validate event against per-tenant contract before upsert
function process(event, tenant) {
  const contract = registry.get(tenant);
  if (!contract.validate(event)) throw new Error('Contract violation');
  iceberg.merge(event, canonicalKey, tenant);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test contract drift across tenants over time?
- What metrics and alerts would you monitor for data quality and lineage?

## Why This Is Asked

Data governance for multiple data sources with contracts ensures safety, compatibility, and faster onboarding of teams at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Data contracts and schema registry
- Versioned Iceberg schemas and per-source isolation
- Idempotent upserts and SCD2 history
- Contract testing and rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-contract check: validate record against contract version
function validate(record, contract) {
  // check required fields
  for (const f of contract.required) {
    if (!(f in record)) return false;
  }
  // type checks
  for (const [k, t] of Object.entries(contract.types)) {
    if (typeof record[k] !== t) return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect contract drift across sources, and what actions would you trigger?
- How would you handle data already ingested when a contract is updated—rollback, versioning, or retroactive validation?
